// Language section: Records (the `type` keyword).
page records { sites = [:reference, :wcl_skill]  title = "Records"
  h1 "Records"

  p "A `type` declares a named record — a fixed set of named, typed fields. Records describe the shape of values, function parameters, and the backing type of every block."

  h2 "Declaring a record type"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
type Dog {
  name: utf8
  age:  u32
}

let rex = Dog { name: "Rex", age: 4u32 }
WCL
  }

  h2 "Type aliases"

  p "`type Name = TypeRef` declares a transparent alias — a readable name for any type, resolved wherever the name is used (transitively, so an alias of an alias works). Constraint decorators on the alias travel with it: every field declared with the alias is validated against them by `wcl check`."
  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
@min(1) @max(65535)
type Port = u16

@non_empty
type Name = utf8

type Service {
  name: Name        # rejects ""
  port: Port        # rejects 0u16 and 70000-ish values
}
WCL
  }

  h2 "extends"

  p "A record may `extends` another, inheriting all its fields. The child type is a structural superset; it satisfies any context that accepts the parent."
  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
type Dog {
  name: utf8
  age:  u32
}

type Pet extends Dog {
  breed: utf8
}
WCL
  }

  callout "Records and blocks" {
    class = ["note"]
    body  = "A record becomes a block kind when decorated with @block(\"kind\") — see Schema & Decorators. The block's labels then bind positionally to fields marked @inline(N)."
  }
}
